#+TITLE: Parallel Windows unit suites share temporary fixture names
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug

* Evidence
Native jobs17693487997 (!507) and17693490276 (!476) ran concurrently on the
same Windows account. Both use C:/msys64/tmp. The core suite reports four
failures: the CRLF fixture reads lone CR contents, another fixture cannot be
deleted because another process holds it, and a SECURELOAD fixture disappears.
The fixture uses process-local RANDOM state, so separate processes can choose
the same names.

* Correction
All three native Windows unit lanes inherit a common unit-test template.
A reusable PowerShell initializer sets TEMP, TMP and TMPDIR for the current
job process to CI_BUILDS_DIR/.clautolisp-unit-tmp/CI_JOB_ID, created after
checkout. An after_script removes only that job's directory when possible.
The directory is outside the checkout, so a leftover locked file cannot break
the next job's get_sources. Real CAD jobs retain the existing shared TEMP
because their orphan/trusted-path journals must survive across jobs.

* Validation
The regression starts two real PowerShell child processes in parallel.
They create the same fixture basename with different contents, verify each
keeps its contents, report distinct paths and PIDs, and exercise cleanup.
The parent environment remains unchanged. Passed on Windows PowerShell5.1
on pfenny and PowerShell7 on macOS. The CI core unit lane runs this regression
before its normal suite. The full Windows unit lanes are rerun in CI to verify integration.
